.TH TLS12 2
.SH NAME
tls12: client, prf, Session \- TLS 1.2 client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "sys.m";
include "keyring.m";
include "tls12.m";
tls12 := load TLS12 TLS12->PATH;
Session: import tls12;

init:      fn(): string;
prf:       fn(secret: array of byte, label: string,
              seed: array of byte, n: int): array of byte;
client:    fn(fd: ref Sys->FD, hostname: string,
              suites: list of int): (ref Session, string);
loadroots: fn(path: string): (list of array of byte, string);
CAPATH:    con "/lib/tls/ca-bundle.pem";

Session: adt {
    cipher: int;
    certs:  list of array of byte;
    err:    string;
    write:  fn(s: self ref Session, buf: array of byte, n: int): int;
    read:   fn(s: self ref Session, buf: array of byte, n: int): int;
    close:  fn(s: self ref Session);
};
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Tls12
is a TLS 1.2 client, built because
.IR ssl3 (2)'s
module speaks only SSLv3 and cannot complete a handshake with any
modern HTTPS host. Its scope is exactly what
.I doc/ssl-modernization-plan.md
measured as sufficient for its real targets, since extended to reach
the ECDSA hosts that make up much of the modern web: TLS 1.2, key
exchange over X25519 or NIST P-256 (secp256r1), server authentication
by RSA or ECDSA (P-256) certificate, and AES-128-GCM or
ChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD record protection \(em the four cipher suites
.B TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256
(16rC02F),
.B TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_CHACHA20_POLY1305
(16rCCA8),
.B TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256
(16rC02B) and
.B TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_CHACHA20_POLY1305
(16rCCA9). The ECDSA/P-256 arithmetic is
.IR p256 (2).
There is still no TLS 1.3, no CBC suite, no session resumption, no
renegotiation and no client certificate support; requests for any of
those fail cleanly rather than negotiating something outside scope.
.PP
The handshake flow, secret derivation and AEAD record construction
are ported from 9front
.RB ( tlshand.c
for the handshake,
.B devtls.c
for the record layer 9front keeps in its kernel), on top of the
primitives
.IR keyring (2)
gained for this plan: HMAC-SHA256, X25519, AES-GCM and
ChaCha20-Poly1305.
.PP
.B Init
must be called once before anything else and returns nil on success
or a diagnostic.
.PP
.B Client
runs a full client handshake over
.IR fd ,
an already-connected TCP stream (normally to port 443), sending
.I hostname
in the SNI extension.
.I Suites
lists the cipher suites to offer in preference order; nil offers both
of the suites above. It returns
.BI (\fIsession\fP,\ nil)
on success or
.BI (nil,\ \fIdiagnostic\fP) .
.PP
The server is authenticated before any secret is derived: its
certificate chain must verify against the trust roots at
.B CAPATH
and name
.I hostname
(via
.IR x509 (2)'s
.BR verify_chain_host ,
which checks issuer/subject chaining, validity dates, intermediates'
CA bit, subjectAltName dNSNames, and every RSA signature), and the
ServerKeyExchange parameters must carry a valid signature by the leaf
certificate's key over both hello randoms \(em tying this key
exchange to this connection and this server. Either failing aborts
the handshake.
.B CAPATH
is a frozen copy of Mozilla's root-CA bundle shipped in the tree;
refresh it from
.B https://curl.se/ca/cacert.pem
when it ages.
.B Loadroots
parses such a PEM bundle into the DER certificate list
.B verify_chain_host
takes (nil path means
.BR CAPATH );
.B client
calls it once and caches the result.
.PP
An established
.B Session
carries the negotiated
.B cipher
and the server's (verified) certificate chain in
.B certs
(DER, leaf first, exactly as received).
.B Write
encrypts and sends
.I n
bytes of application data, returning
.I n
or \-1;
.B read
returns up to
.I n
decrypted bytes, 0 at a clean close (the peer's
.B close_notify
alert), or \-1 on error. After any \-1,
.B err
holds a sticky diagnostic and the session is dead.
.B Close
sends a
.B close_notify
alert; closing the file descriptor itself remains the caller's job,
as does dialling (see
.IR dial (2)
\(em and note the connection server gap recorded in
.IR dialtest (1):
in this emu instance names do not resolve, so callers dial literal
IPs while SNI carries the hostname string).
.PP
.B Prf
is RFC 5246 section 5's pseudorandom function (P_SHA256 over
HMAC-SHA256), returning
.I n
bytes derived from
.IR secret ,
.I label
and
.IR seed .
The handshake uses it internally; it stays exported for
.IR tls12prftest (1)
and any other protocol code needing the TLS 1.2 KDF.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/crypt/tls12.b
.br
.B /module/tls12.m
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tls12test (1),
.IR tls12prftest (1),
.IR keyring (2),
.IR ssl3 (2),
.IR dial (2),
.I doc/ssl-modernization-plan.md
in the source tree.
.SH BUGS
There is no revocation checking of any kind \(em no CRLs, no OCSP \(em
so a compromised-and-revoked certificate verifies here until it
expires. Name constraints and certificate policies are not processed.
Trust is only as fresh as the frozen bundle at
.BR CAPATH ;
nothing updates it automatically, and a stale bundle eventually means
spurious verification failures (missing new roots) rather than
anything unsafe. Certificate keys may be RSA
(sha1/256/384/512WithRSAEncryption) or ECDSA on P-256
(ecdsa-with-SHA256/384/512); other curves and key types fail cleanly.
The system clock is trusted for validity periods.
